A colour that would not archive left a window permanently unflushable.

THE DOCUMENT WINDOW WAS PRESENTED EXACTLY ONCE. Swift Publisher opens a template, draws it, hands
it to the compositor, and from then on draws forever into a buffer nobody is ever shown: its half
of the screen keeps the one frame it got. Measured before: 33469 dirty marks, 20573 of them that
window, 1756 draw traversals, ONE present.

IT IS TWO DEFECTS AND THEY MEET IN THE MIDDLE.

ONE, THE ENCODER WAS MISSING A HALF THE DECODER ALWAYS HAD. -[NSColor initWithCoder:] reads a
non-keyed archive: a uint8 space code, then the components, per space. -[NSColor encodeWithCoder:]
handled only the keyed case and RAISED for everything else, so a colour could be read back but
never written. Swift Publisher archives colours with the typedstream, and it raised 1094 times in
one run. The encoder added here writes exactly what that decoder reads, in the same two calls, for
all six space codes. A catalog colour encodes its RESOLVED colour and not self, because self would
recurse until the stack ran out. An unknown space still raises, so a colour the decoder could not
read is never silently written.

TWO, AND THIS IS THE ONE THAT DID THE DAMAGE. -[NSWindow displayIfNeeded] brackets its descent:

    [self disableFlushWindow];      _flushDisabled++
    [_backgroundView displayIfNeeded];
    [self enableFlushWindow];       _flushDisabled--

_flushDisabled is a COUNTER, not a flag. The raise above unwound out of the middle of that, the
decrement never ran, and -flushWindow tests _flushDisabled > 0 and returns early. So ONE exception
anywhere in ANY draw, in any application, puts a window beyond presentation for the rest of the
session, and nothing says so: the window keeps answering events and keeps drawing. Both display
methods now bracket with @try/@finally, all four sites, so the enable happens on the way out
whatever the draw did. The exception still propagates to NSApplication, which is where it was
already reported.

MEASURED AFTER, same scenario, LOOKED AT:

    exceptions            1094 -> 0
    descents, window 36      0 -> 7, 855 views visited
    presents, window 36      1 -> 3 and climbing

Swift Publisher now runs past the freeze and reaches its own trial licence dialog, which is the
next thing in front of the document and is the application asking, not the port failing.

Task #245.
--- a/AppKit/NSColor.subproj/NSColor.m
+++ b/AppKit/NSColor.subproj/NSColor.m
@@ -118,9 +118,70 @@
         }
 
     } else {
-        [NSException raise: NSInvalidArgumentException
-                    format: @"%@ can not encodeWithCoder:%@", [self class],
-                            [coder class]];
+        /*
+         * THE DECODER HAS ALWAYS READ THIS FORMAT; only the encoder was missing, so every
+         * non-keyed archive of a colour raised instead of writing one. A space code, then the
+         * components, in the two separate calls -initWithCoder: reads them back with.
+         *
+         * WHAT IT COST, and why it is worth more than an archive: a raise here unwinds out of
+         * -drawRect:, and -[NSWindow displayIfNeeded] brackets its descent with
+         * -disableFlushWindow / -enableFlushWindow. The enable is skipped, _flushDisabled never
+         * returns to zero, and from that moment -flushWindow returns early forever: the window
+         * goes on drawing and is never presented again. Swift Publisher raised it 1094 times and
+         * its document window was handed to the compositor exactly once. Task #245.
+         */
+        NSColorSpaceName spaceName = [self colorSpaceName];
+        uint8_t space;
+
+        if ([spaceName isEqualToString: NSCalibratedRGBColorSpace] ||
+            [spaceName isEqualToString: NSDeviceRGBColorSpace]) {
+            CGFloat r, g, b, a;
+            float values[4];
+
+            space = [spaceName isEqualToString: NSCalibratedRGBColorSpace] ? 1 : 2;
+            [self getRed: &r green: &g blue: &b alpha: &a];
+            values[0] = r, values[1] = g, values[2] = b, values[3] = a;
+            [coder encodeValuesOfObjCTypes: "c", &space];
+            [coder encodeValuesOfObjCTypes: "ffff", &values[0], &values[1],
+                                           &values[2], &values[3]];
+        } else if ([spaceName isEqualToString: NSCalibratedWhiteColorSpace] ||
+                   [spaceName isEqualToString: NSDeviceWhiteColorSpace]) {
+            CGFloat w, a;
+            float values[2];
+
+            space = [spaceName isEqualToString: NSCalibratedWhiteColorSpace] ? 3 : 4;
+            [self getWhite: &w alpha: &a];
+            values[0] = w, values[1] = a;
+            [coder encodeValuesOfObjCTypes: "c", &space];
+            [coder encodeValuesOfObjCTypes: "ff", &values[0], &values[1]];
+        } else if ([spaceName isEqualToString: NSDeviceCMYKColorSpace]) {
+            CGFloat c, m, y, k, a;
+            float values[5];
+
+            space = 5;
+            [self getCyan: &c magenta: &m yellow: &y black: &k alpha: &a];
+            values[0] = c, values[1] = m, values[2] = y, values[3] = k,
+            values[4] = a;
+            [coder encodeValuesOfObjCTypes: "c", &space];
+            [coder encodeValuesOfObjCTypes: "fffff", &values[0], &values[1],
+                                           &values[2], &values[3], &values[4]];
+        } else if ([spaceName isEqualToString: NSNamedColorSpace]) {
+            NSColorListName catalogName = [self catalogNameComponent];
+            NSColorName colorName = [self colorNameComponent];
+            /* The resolved colour, NOT self: a catalog colour encoding itself here would recurse
+             * until the stack ran out. */
+            NSColor *resolved =
+                    [self colorUsingColorSpaceName: NSCalibratedRGBColorSpace];
+
+            space = 6;
+            [coder encodeValuesOfObjCTypes: "c", &space];
+            [coder encodeValuesOfObjCTypes: "@@@", &catalogName, &colorName,
+                                           &resolved];
+        } else {
+            [NSException raise: NSInvalidArgumentException
+                        format: @"%@ can not encodeWithCoder:%@ in space %@",
+                                [self class], [coder class], spaceName];
+        }
     }
 }
 
